It received less funding than other federal law enforcement agencies, had fewer employees, and frequently came under attack by the country's powerful gun lobby, the National Rifle Association. The ATF's role as the nation's enforcer of gun laws meant it was often accused of trampling on citizens' Second Amendment rights, the amendment in the US Constitution that enshrines the rights of Americans to bear arms. In the early 1990s, the ATF was in such dire financial straits that agents in charge of divisional officers began issuing fines to other agents if they were late for meetings, just so they had some money they could use to purchase necessary equipment. The ATF undertook its own investigations, but was also often called,

